UPDATE public.guided_drills SET question_types=ARRAY[$gd$Weaken$gd$]::text[], question_features=ARRAY[$gd$Anti-Causal$gd$]::text[], instruction_card=$gd${"left_md": "**Anti-Causal** arguments attempt to argue that \"X makes no difference\", \"X didn't help\", or \"it's not resulting from X, but Y\". Their evidence is usually that things seem to be the same, or worse, without X.\n\n# EXAMPLES\n\nAnn and Jerry both studied LSAT for 3 months, but only Ann added a mindfulness regimen to her studying. Since they both ended up with similar LSAT scores, apparently the mindfulness regimen didn't do anything for Ann.\n\nWhen President Knowles took office, the unemployment rate was 9%. Shortly after her stimulus bill passed, the unemployment rate hit 10%. Clearly, her stimulus bill did nothing to help employment.\n\nAnti-Causal arguments are vulnerable to several common objections:\n\n- **different initial reference points**  \n  the mindfulness _did_ help Ann, because she went from a 140 to a 160, whereas Jerry just went from a 150 to 160.\n\n- **would have been even worse without**  \n  the stimulus bill _did_ help, because the economy was in such a free-fall that if it hadn't been for the stimulus bill, the unemployment would have been at like 12%\n\n- **overlooked consideration**  \n  even though it looks like the stimulus bill did nothing because unemployment continued to rise in its immediate aftermath, that's because it takes several months before stimulus can manifest any changes in employment figures.", "right_md": "Try these three questions that have an Anti-Causal vibe to them.\n\nThe conclusion will sound like it's saying,  \n\"So, X doesn't cause Y\"\n\nIf we're objecting to these arguments, we need to find a way to accept the evidence but still find a way to argue that X _could_ cause Y."}$gd$::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug=$gd$w9-08-anti-causal-hard-mini-set$gd$;
UPDATE public.guided_drills SET question_types=ARRAY[$gd$Weaken$gd$]::text[], question_features=ARRAY[$gd$Anti-Causal$gd$]::text[], instruction_card=$gd${"left_md": "In many **Anti-Causal** arguments, the author doesn't believe that \"X causes / explains Y\", because the author's premise supposedly makes that causal relationship seem implausible or wrong.\n\n_for example_ -- Timmy says that his love of sushi was caused by his upbringing. However, this must be false, since he did not grow up in a Japanese household.\n\nIn objecting to these arguments, we're essentially trying to restore the idea that, \"yes, X _does_ cause Y\" (Timmy's upbringing _is_ what explains his love of sushi) while reconciling that with the author's concern.\n\nEven though your premise is true,  \nit still _is_ the case that X caused Y,  \nbecause ... ?\n\nEven though Timmy didn't grow up in a Japanese household,  \nit still _is_ the case that his upbringing caused his love of sushi,  \nbecause ... ?", "right_md": "# Try these 6 problems untimed.\n\nFocus on finding the anti-causal features.\n\nThe authors will be thinking, \"How can this Cause/Effect relationship be true if fact X seems to go against it?\" (fact X will normally feel like something showing Cause w/o Effect or Effect w/o Cause)\n\nSo our job in thinking about these problems is to figure out we can reconcile fact X with the Cause/Effect relationship.\n\nSet up your thinking this way to respond to their evidence:\n\n- A _does_ cause B (or A _did_ cause B)\n- X is just true because ... ?\n\nCorrect weakening ideas on Anti-Causal arguments will usually feel like they're \"**making an excuse**\" for the premise, helping us to see it in a new light that allows us to still believe in the Cause/Effect relationship being disputed.\n\n---"}$gd$::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug=$gd$w8-03-anti-causal-set-3$gd$;
UPDATE public.guided_drills SET question_types=ARRAY[$gd$Weaken$gd$,$gd$Strengthen$gd$]::text[], question_features=ARRAY[$gd$Assumes Similarity$gd$]::text[], instruction_card=$gd${"left_md": "## Every comparison hides an assumed sameness\n\nIn the world of **Comparisons**, authors lean on a few recurring assumptions:\n- What was true in the **past** is still true **today**.\n- What is true **today** will be true in the **future**.\n- What was true for **this** group, person, or thing is true for **that** one.\n\nWhenever an argument reasons across time or across cases, it is assuming the two sides are similar enough to carry the conclusion.\n\nThat gives you both attack and support:\n- **Weaken** by pointing out a salient difference between the two sides.\n- **Strengthen** by adding a similarity or affirming the assumed one.", "right_md": "## Try these 5, untimed\n\nGo for accuracy and pattern recognition, not speed.\n\nFor each argument:\n- Name the comparison. What two things, groups, or moments is the author treating as alike?\n- Find where that sameness is assumed rather than proven.\n- Prephrase the gap: \"this only works if the two sides are similar in the way that matters.\"\n\nThen judge the answers:\n- A weakener surfaces a **difference** that breaks the link.\n- A strengthener shores up the **similarity**.\n- Wrong answers often raise a difference that does not matter to the conclusion, or restate the comparison without testing it."}$gd$::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug=$gd$w7-03-assumes-similarity-mini-set$gd$;
UPDATE public.guided_drills SET question_types=ARRAY[$gd$Weaken$gd$,$gd$Strengthen$gd$]::text[], question_features=ARRAY[$gd$Assumes Similarity$gd$]::text[], instruction_card=$gd${"left_md": "## Every comparison hides an assumed sameness\n\nIn the world of **Comparisons**, authors lean on a few recurring assumptions:\n- What was true in the **past** is still true **today**.\n- What is true **today** will be true in the **future**.\n- What was true for **this** group, person, or thing is true for **that** one.\n\nWhen an author reasons across time or across cases, the argument only holds if the two sides are similar enough to carry the conclusion. That assumed similarity is the pressure point.\n\n- **Weaken** by pointing out a salient difference between the two sides.\n- **Strengthen** by adding a similarity or affirming the assumed one.", "right_md": "## Try these 7, untimed\n\nGo for accuracy and pattern recognition, not speed.\n\nFor each argument:\n- Name the comparison. What two things, groups, or moments is the author treating as alike?\n- Find where that sameness is assumed rather than proven.\n- Prephrase the gap: \"this only works if the two sides are similar in the way that matters.\"\n\nThen judge the answers:\n- A weakener surfaces a **difference** that breaks the link.\n- A strengthener shores up the **similarity**.\n- Wrong answers often raise a difference that does not matter, or simply restate the comparison without testing it."}$gd$::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug=$gd$w8-02-assumes-similarity-set-1$gd$;
UPDATE public.guided_drills SET question_types=ARRAY[$gd$Principle-Strengthen$gd$]::text[], question_features=ARRAY[$gd$Principle-Strengthen$gd$]::text[], instruction_card=$gd${"left_md": "## This set has 7 **Principle-Strengthen**\n## questions from tests 55-59\n\nAll of these examples are the much-more common style of Principle-Strengthen in which the _answers_ are principles. They often feel more like **Sufficient Assumption** than Strengthen, because the correct answer is usually a conditional rule that is triggered by the Evidence and results in the Conclusion.\n\nBut the question stem is technically a Strengthen task (which answer _most_ justifies), so we're not supposed to ask ourselves, \"Does this answer prove the conclusion\", even though in many cases it will. In some rare cases, though, the correct answer could be as weakly worded as a Necessary Assumption.\n\nNevertheless, our primary approach to these is to expect a conditional logic answer, so we pre-phrase by articulating an \"IF premise, THEN conclusion\" reasoning move.\n\nMost of the answers will be conditionals. If so, we want to be very **stubborn with the outcome** . The right side of the rule should match the conclusion (or at least sound like wording that would strongly support the conclusion). We want to be **flexible with the trigger** .\n\nOther answers feel more like comparative ideas that help _Weigh Tradeoffs_ or establish priorities. When arguments seem to be balancing competing ideas (a pro and a con / this obligation with that obligation), then when the author issues her judgment in the conclusion, she's revealing whether she cares more about the good thing or the bad thing, whether she cares more about this obligation or that obligation.\n\nGiven this argument, we'd anticipate a conditional answer\n_Billy lied to our customers. Thus he shouldn't be on our staff anymore._\n\n(A) A person can be on the staff only if they are consistently truthful with customers\n\nGiven this argument, we might see a weighing-tradeoffs answer\n_Billy is extremely dependable, although she lied to our customers. Clearly, she shouldn't be on our staff._\n\n(A) Dependability is a less important trait in a staff member than is honesty.", "right_md": "## Try these 7 problems, **untimed**\n\n- Once you find the Conclusion and identify the main Supporting Idea(s), you will generally want to articulate an, \"If [premise], then [conclusion]\" linking idea.\n\n- If, otherwise, you felt like the argument was grappling with competing interests, then articulate how the author is weighing tradeoffs:  \"she cares more about X than about Y\".\n\n- Try to get rid of a lot answers quickly because they don't have any good matching language for the conclusion, or because the matching language for the conclusion is being put on the **left** of the conditional arrow.\n\n- Correct answers will often, if not usually, be written in contrapositive form, so be willing to try that move. If you read \"should be on staff only if consistently truthful\", then you have to make your brain say, \"Okay, this is saying _if you're_ **_not_** _consistently truthful, should_ **_not_** _on staff_ \"."}$gd$::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug=$gd$w4-01-principle-strengthen-set-2$gd$;
UPDATE public.guided_drills SET question_types=ARRAY[$gd$Principle-Strengthen$gd$,$gd$Application$gd$]::text[], question_features=ARRAY[$gd$Principle-Strengthen$gd$]::text[], instruction_card=$gd${"left_md": "Find the gap between a Rule and its Application\non these five **Principle-Strengthen** questions\n\n_example_\nPRINCIPLE: Lies are justified if they do more good than harm.\nAPPLICATION: Jason falsely claimed that Sharon has a driver's license to spare her getting mocked by her friends. Since he did manage to spare her the mockery, he was justified in lying.\n\nIn order to use this Rule to prove that a lie was justified, we have to establish the trigger:\n_the lie did more good than harm_\n\nSo the correct answer would need to convince us that \"keeping Sharon from getting mocked = did more good than harm\".\n\n(Note: there are two styles of Principle-Strengthen question:  _Principles in Stimulus vs. Principle in Answers_ . The latter kind is more than 10x more common, but the former kind shows up more on modern tests than it used to)", "right_md": "- Think about each Principle as having some criteria that must be met to trigger the rule, and having an outcome that matches up with the Application's conclusion.\n\n- The Application will (almost?) always conclude the right side of the Principle.\n\n- What we're being tested on is how the Evidence in the Application paragraph failed to clearly trigger the legal definition of the Principle.\n\n- Think about which part(s) of the Principle's trigger wasn't clearly established, and keep that mantra in your head as you consider answer choices."}$gd$::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug=$gd$w4-02-principle-strengthen-application-set-1$gd$;
UPDATE public.guided_drills SET question_types=ARRAY[$gd$Flaw$gd$]::text[], question_features=ARRAY[$gd$Famous Flaws$gd$]::text[], instruction_card=$gd${"left_md": "## The flaw is usually one of the famous ten\n\nMedium Flaw questions reward pattern recognition. The author makes a reasoning move that breaks in a predictable way, and the LSAT reuses the same handful of breaks again and again. We call these the **Famous Flaws**.\n\nThe ten that show up most:\n- **Necessary vs. Sufficient** (a rule used backwards or negated)\n- **Causation** (correlation treated as cause, reversed cause, third factor)\n- **Sampling** (an unrepresentative group)\n- **Equivocation** (one word, two meanings)\n- **Percent vs. Amount** (a rate confused for a count)\n- **Ad Hominem** (attacking the source, not the claim)\n- **Circular** (the conclusion smuggled into the evidence)\n- **Whole vs. Part**\n- **Appeal to Authority / Popularity**\n- **False Choice** (only two options when more exist)\n\nAt the medium level the trap answers also describe famous flaws. A wrong answer names a real flaw that this argument simply never made.", "right_md": "## Try these 5, then time them\n\nSet a timer for **8 minutes** (1.5x = 12, 2x = 16). Get an answer to all five, and flag any that felt like a low-confidence guess.\n\nFor each argument:\n- Find the conclusion, then ask what the author had to assume to get there.\n- Name the broken move in your own words before you read the answers.\n- Match your prephrase to the famous-flaw list.\n\nWhen you read the answers:\n- A choice can describe a real flaw and still be wrong because the argument never made it. Ask, did this argument actually do this?\n- Some correct answers state the flaw in plain, specific language, not the textbook phrasing. Do not wait for the classic wording.\n\nAfter the timer, take a slow untimed second pass before you confirm. Note any flaw you keep missing."}$gd$::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug=$gd$w8-01-famous-flaws-medium-set-1$gd$;
UPDATE public.guided_drills SET question_types=ARRAY[$gd$Parallel Flaw$gd$]::text[], question_features=ARRAY[$gd$Famous Flaws$gd$]::text[], instruction_card=$gd${"left_md": "## Same broken move, new clothes\n\nThis set blends two tasks that lean on the same skill: spotting the **Famous Flaw** in an argument, then finding another argument that breaks the *same* way.\n\nOn **Parallel Flaw**, the correct answer matches the structure of the error, not the topic. A stimulus about vitamins and a correct answer about city budgets can be a perfect match if both commit the same Necessary vs. Sufficient slip.\n\nThe flaws you will see most here:\n- **Necessary vs. Sufficient** (a conditional rule used backwards or negated)\n- **Causation** (correlation read as cause)\n- **Whole vs. Part** and **Part vs. Whole**\n- **Percent vs. Amount**\n- **Equivocation**\n\nName the flaw in abstract terms and the matching gets much easier.", "right_md": "## Work the set untimed first\n\nFor each argument:\n- Find the conclusion and the support, then state the broken move as a rule: \"the author treats a necessary condition as if it were sufficient.\"\n- On **Parallel Flaw**, carry that abstract sentence to the answers and find the one that commits the identical move. Ignore the subject matter.\n- On straight **Flaw**, pick the choice that describes that same move, and watch for traps that name a different famous flaw the argument never made.\n\nCheck answers and explanations as you go. When two answers feel close on a Parallel question, the tie-breaker is usually whether the conclusion type matches: a prediction parallels a prediction, a recommendation parallels a recommendation."}$gd$::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug=$gd$w8-04-parallel-flaw-famous-flaws-set-2$gd$;
UPDATE public.guided_drills SET question_types=ARRAY[$gd$Point at Issue / Agree-Disagree$gd$]::text[], question_features=ARRAY[$gd$Timed Agree/Disagree$gd$]::text[], instruction_card=$gd${"left_md": "## Find the one claim they actually clash on\n\nAgree / Disagree questions (also called Point at Issue) give you two speakers. The correct answer is the single statement where one speaker would **affirm** it and the other would **deny** it.\n\nMost wrong answers fail one of two tests:\n- Only one speaker addresses it, so we cannot know the other's view.\n- Both speakers would actually take the same side.\n\nYou are not hunting for conclusion versus support here. You are mapping what each person commits to, then finding the one point of genuine collision.", "right_md": "## Try these 5 in 8 minutes\n\n(1.5x = 12, 2x = 16). Read each person's paragraph for what they claim, not for argument structure.\n\nThen, for each claim the first speaker made, ask:\n- Did the second speaker **agree**, **disagree**, or give **no comment**?\n- If no one stated it outright, would the first speaker's reasoning force a view the second speaker rejects?\n\nThe right answer is the claim where one says yes and the other says no. Run each choice through a simple check: could both speakers fill in a clear \"I agree\" or \"I disagree\"? If either has to shrug, that answer is out."}$gd$::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug=$gd$w5-08-agree-disagree-set-2$gd$;
UPDATE public.guided_drills SET question_types=ARRAY[$gd$Mixed LR question types$gd$]::text[], question_features=ARRAY[$gd$Conditional Logic$gd$]::text[], instruction_card=$gd${"left_md": "## When to reach for the diagram\n\nThis set mixes question types, but they share one trigger: **conditional logic** dense enough that diagramming pays off. The types that most often reward a diagram:\n- **Must Be True** and **Most Supported**\n- **Sufficient Assumption**\n- **Parallel** and **Parallel Flaw**\n- **Principle-Strengthen** and **Principle-Conform**\n\nThe move is always the same. Translate each conditional into an arrow, chain the arrows that connect, and read off what must follow. The contrapositive is half your toolkit, so write it down.\n\nOn **Flaw** questions with conditional logic, expect the author to abuse a rule with an illegal **reversal** or **negation**: the Necessary vs. Sufficient flaw.", "right_md": "## Try these 7, on paper\n\nFrom tests 65, 71, and June 2007. Keep a pen moving.\n\nFor each stimulus:\n- Spot the conditional **trigger words** (if, only if, unless, no, all, none) and diagram them.\n- Write the **contrapositive** of any rule you diagram.\n- Look for conditionals that **chain** together, then read the chain end to end.\n\nJudging answers:\n- On inference questions, the answer must be forced by the chain, not merely consistent with it.\n- On **Sufficient Assumption**, find the missing link that completes the chain from evidence to conclusion.\n- On **Flaw / Parallel Flaw**, name the illegal move (reversal or negation) and match it exactly."}$gd$::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug=$gd$w12-03-conditional-logic-harder-set-4$gd$;
